> The selection type for the first candidate in round 1 is based on the
> results of some exhaustive "off-line" calculations[1]. All the subsequent
> rounds use a heuristical deterministic algorithm[2] to choose a possible
> candidate from the remaining pattern space[**]. To have some diversity the
> schema for the very first choice uses random transposed and random
> substituted colors (symbols), without loss of generality.

All of the heavy duty algorithms I've used suggest using AABBC as the
first guess.

> I am sure that with exhaustive searches over all rounds one can optimise any
> solution further, though given the simplicity of the algorithm it seems
> quite appropriate for Nethack's Mastermind.

Yeah, such is the case. The next step is an exhaustive search to
minimize the largest solution subset (Knuth's) or to maximize the
number of solution subsets (what I call "Breadth", but is easily also
called Bartooi's)

You might like to read about my experiments with Mastermind here:
http://sartak.katron.org/nh/mastermind/ -- it also includes a C solver;
you can choose which algorithm it uses, or solve for all tunes instead
of solving interactively.

> [**] Solutions had been suggested to use the first possible candidate, but
> that approach had seemed to me to be suboptimal (and turned out to be so) due
> to the way the patterns are distributed; the results are the ones depicted in
> Table 2.

This is "Shapiro's algorithm" (using the first-lexicographic candidate
consistent with known results). I call it "Knuth 1" on the page but
that name is slightly misleading, but it does segue into the "real"
Knuth's algorithm (which I call Knuth 4).

